as the row about allegations of sleaze  continue the government is focusing  its attention elsewhere last night the  new domestic violence bill was passed by  parliament  promising new protection for victims  well let's speak now to the safeguarding  minister victoria atkins who joins me  uh this morning um are you confident  that the bill having passed will  completely protect uh those in  situations of domestic violence we know  that the numbers  have risen through uh lockdown and those  people now coming out  of lockdown will they be 100 protected  well this this bill is a truly landmark  bill we know that there are 2.3 million  adult victims of domestic abuse in this  country  and of course children who live within  abusive households as well  and this bill first of all puts a  definition of domestic abuse in many  forms it can take  but which is important for commissioning  of services at local level but it does  so much more than that  we are making real meaningful changes to  the ways in which family courts operate  because we know that abusers sadly can  often try to use the family courts to  continue the abuse  we're prohibiting non-fatal  strangulation we're prohibiting  uh the use of threats to use revenge  porn we're  making changes to the so-called rough  sex defense clarifying the law on that  so there's a huge amount of work that  we're doing  to help victims of domestic abuse and  also inc  critically to stop perpetrators we're  seeing record levels of investment under  this government  in programmes to stop perpetrators  because we've got to stop this cycle of  abuse this  perpetrator is going from one  relationship to another  and i think this is a real body of work  that the bill provides the foundations  for but we're very conscious  that the hard work starts now  implementing it the bill however has  received criticism  over how it deals with with stalking  after the amendment  requiring offenders to be monitored on a  national  register was dropped why would that be  dropped i mean surely  a register actually keeping tabs on  offenders  is going to help those that are being  abused well in fairness i think this  bill has received  uh support from across the political  spectrum  i know you're focusing on that  particular point but genuinely this has  been a  real um it's been parliament at its best  i would say because  we've listened to ideas and suggestions  and where  we've been able to where we think they  will work we've absolutely listened and  then  acted on this particular measure we we  looked very very carefully at it  it's very technical it's about how we  manage serial offenders uh and also  the most violent and sexual offenders  outside of the domestic abuse context as  well as  within a domestic abuse context but we  have spoken we've worked really closely  with baroness royal who was the  peer bringing this amendment forward we  have um  very much understood some of the  concerns that have been raised we're  going to improve guidance we're i'm  setting out a new framework  we're finding ways to address the  concerns that have been raised in the  house  but we just we weren't convinced that  this particular amendment was the way to  fix that but of course we will continue  to keep this under review  a national register is not a a good idea  i mean many lay people will think that  it makes perfect sense  yeah so the problem with registers um is  that  uh if someone what we don't want is  people  falling between the gaps so we have a  really really um  thorough system of managing a serial  offenders both violence and sexual  offenders as i say both in and out of a  domestic  context 85 000 offenders are managed  under this system  called mapper and um the  what we want to do they will be stalkers  but they will also be sadly sexual  offenders they'll  they will be um very very violent  offenders and  uh what we're doing is is looking at the  system as a whole we are very conscious  that  uh the some parts of the system have  outlived their usefulness the visa  system is more than 20 years old  so next year we will be piloting a brand  new digital  uh forum which we believe will really  answer some of these concerns that have  been raised but the critical point about  managing stalkers  is about the decisions that are taken at  local level to manage the risk of these  people  and that's what we need to focus on  rather than we think setting up a new  register  let's move on to uh the prime minister  regarding um  allegations of corruption and saying  that there's nothing  to see um when the electoral  commissioner said  it has reasonable grounds to launch an  investigation  surely shouldn't the prime minister just  actually say  who paid for the refurbishments in the  first instance rather than allowing all  these investigations to be launched  well the prime minister has set out uh  his case very clearly actually in the  house of commons on wednesday he was the  most direct  question though has he in the comments  he didn't answer that direct question  well he's answered the questions that  were put to him and and  um i know that he was asked yes or no  did he pay for the refurbishments and he  didn't answer yes or no  well as i say the prime minister has  answered those questions as you know i'm  on here to talk about the domestic abuse  bill  but the prime minister has answered the  questions he has  very much given his answers to the house  and i i do wonder you know here i am  today  we've got this amazing piece of  legislation that will help  as i say millions of people across the  country and it would be nice if just  today we can focus on that well  we have we have that's how we started  the interview we have asked you about  that  but also people people are wanting to  have answers to questions that the prime  minister isn't simply answering  well i don't accept that as i say he's  answered questions in the house of  commons  we had the we've had the announcement of  uh lord guyt being appointed as the  independent adviser  uh and of course you know these reviews  these inquiries will  uh take their path but the prime  minister has answered to the house i i  don't really feel i can add to that  but but ultimately as downing street has  said the prime minister will be the  the ultimate arbiter of of any inquiry  uh that's like marking your own homework  isn't it  well no the way that we work our  democratic system in this country is  that the prime minister  the prime minister of the day wins the  general election uh and  he or she appoints the government and  then he or she is answerable at  the dispatch box in parliament and of  course in due course at the ballot box  to the electorate  and so he must have control over who is  in his government he's answerable for  their actions it seems to me  the consequences of some of the  suggestions that have been flung around  in the press this week  haven't really been thought through i'm  not sure anyone wants  an unelected uh unaccountable advisor  impeccable and with all of the integrity  that  uh lord guy has i'm not sure that that  is an addition to our constitution that  uh  we want to walk into without fully  understanding the consequences  we do know that sir alex allen quit the  role after the pm  essentially overrode his findings  regarding pretty patel  and bullying how do we know that the  prime minister is going to listen to any  findings of  any investigation there's some nine  investigations at the moment  well first and foremost this process has  been transparent  uh and i think you know in terms of the  previous investigation that has been  settled the  home secretary apologized uh on national  television  um for the uh findings of that review  and so again i'm just i'm not sure i can  add anything more to what we've ignored  it  the prime minister ignored it well  namely that  um the you know the the prime minister  has to have  uh the control of the government has to  have the  confidence that his ministers are the  ministers he wants to  uh exercise the incredible  responsibilities that  ministers have in government and that  seems to me that that has always been  the case and must continue to be the  case  minister thank you very much for  speaking to us this morning thank you
 

